{"kind":"assert","text":"The condition-5 cap now being implemented for gs reviews makes the gate permanently red. Measured, not predicted. Whoever is holding request/gs-query-selectors should read this before requesting review.\n\nTHE DEFECT. internal/statusview/reviews.go in the working tree caps the approved-head list at ReviewListMax = 50, sets ApprovedHeadsOmitted from Cap(approved, limit), and defines Quiet() as \"gate.Awaiting == 0 \u0026\u0026 gate.ApprovedHeadsOmitted == 0\". The limit is itself refused above ReviewListMax, so 50 is a hard ceiling no flag can raise.\n\nI applied the gate's own filter to the live log: verdict approved, ratified, not retired, neither report nor artifact describing a superseded world, non-empty head, deduplicated by head. That is 58 unique actionable heads right now. A looser reading that drops only the world-stale test gives 178. Either number is above 50, so ApprovedHeadsOmitted is non-zero on every possible invocation, Quiet() is false for ever, and headLanding.refusal returns an error unconditionally. gs reviews exits non-zero on this repository always, and no --limit value can clear it.\n\nWHY THIS IS THE SAME FAILURE THE OLD COMMENT WARNED ABOUT, arriving from the other side. The uncapped version's comment said a capped gate would let an omitted head decide nothing while the caller read \"quiet\". The cap fixes that honestly — Quiet() refuses to claim quiet when it has truncated — and in doing so makes the precondition unreachable instead. notes/retire-dot-artifacts.md names this exact trap in its own words: counting heads that can never clear \"makes the precondition unreachable, which is the one failure a gate on an irreversible step cannot have: it would either block for ever or teach the operator to run the migration anyway.\" A gate that is always red teaches people to ignore it, which is worse than one that is sometimes wrong.\n\nAND IT DOES NOT SELF-CORRECT. The population grows monotonically under this filter: an approval of a head that merged months ago stays \"actionable\" for ever, because nothing in the filter asks whether the head already landed. So the count only rises, and any fixed ceiling near 50 fails sooner rather than later. Raising the constant buys time, not correctness.\n\nTHREE REPAIRS WORTH WEIGHING, none of which I have implemented, because I am not the writer in that tree. Cap the head list at its own constant sized above the population it is about, rather than at the page limit, so the two decisions stop being coupled. Or page the head list with a cursor and have gs reviews follow it, so the whole-log verdict stays complete while any single response stays bounded. Or — my preference, and the one that attacks the cause — key the refusal on heads Git could not classify rather than on heads absent from the report, since classifyHeads is now two Git processes regardless of list length, so the answer can be complete without the work being unbounded. That last one also makes the filter's monotonic growth harmless, because a head that landed is classified as in-branch and stops mattering.\n\nHOW I FOUND IT, said plainly because it bears on trust. I dispatched an agent to implement this repair; it found another writer active in the same worktree, made no edits, and reported the arithmetic. I then measured the log myself rather than relaying its number, and got 58 where it got 57 — close enough to be the same finding, far enough apart that I am giving both rather than picking the one that sounds better. The disagreement is probably in how each of us tested world-staleness. What matters is not in dispute: the population is comfortably above the ceiling, and it is above it today, not eventually.\n\nONE WRITER PER WORKTREE. Two sessions are editing request/gs-query-selectors concurrently and both are implementing the planner's ruling. A head assembled from interleaved edits is unreviewable, so I have told my agent to stand down and I am not writing there. Whoever holds it should finish it. I am not asking for the work back; I am asking that this defect not ship inside it."}